<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dream Pool Essays</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1088</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Dream Torrent Essays, Meng Xi Bi Tan, Brush Talks from a Dream Brook, Brush Talks from Dream Brook</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dream Pool Essays (Meng Xi Bi Tan, in full Brush Talks from a Dream Brook) is an encyclopedic book by the Chinese polymath and statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a>, published in 1088 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>. Shen compiled it in forced retirement on his estate near modern Zhenjiang and named it after the estate. Its observations range over medicine, mathematics, astronomy, optics, engineering, metallurgy, early archaeology and fossils, from which Shen reasoned that climates had changed. It holds the first record of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> used for navigation and the first description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. Originally 30 chapters, it was reorganised into 26 in an edition of 1166.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> (1031–1095) had been a government official and military general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> until the chancellor Cai Que had him impeached, wrongly holding him responsible for a defeat by the Tangut-led Western Xia dynasty in 1081 during the Song–Xia wars. He wrote the book in retirement and relative isolation on his garden estate, the Dream Brook. Of the title he is quoted as saying, &quot;Because I had only my writing brush and ink slab to converse with, I call it Brush Talks.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The 584 paragraphs are grouped by subject, among them official life and the court, literature and art, military matters, anecdotes, divination and folklore, mathematics, astronomy and the calendar, meteorology, geology, geography and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a>, medicine, botany and zoology, archaeology, philology and music. The text holds that there is no contradiction between technology and Confucian precepts. Shen describes the first drydock recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sets out the idea of true north in magnetic declination, and explains to the director of the astronomical observatory that the sun and moon are spheres, as the moon's phases show.</p><p>Other passages describe a swarm of predatory insects that destroyed a pest of the crops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qingzhou">Qingzhou</a> in the Yuan-Feng period (1078–1085), lightning that melted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mouths of lacquered vessels without scorching the lacquer, methods of forging swords, and the proportions of buildings in Yu Hao's Timberwork Manual. A passage called Strange Happenings tells of a bright object like a pearl seen hovering at night over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> in the reign of Emperor Renzong, for which people built a Pearl Pavilion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book was already quoted in a work of 1095, a sign that it was being widely printed before Shen's death. The historian Chen Dengyuan points out that much of Shen's writing was probably purged under the minister Cai Jing; only six of his books remain, four of them much altered. The single surviving copy of the 1166 edition is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; a Chinese reprint followed in 1305, and an edition of 1631 regrouped the work into three broad chapters.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a>'s Science and Civilization in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from 1954, carries many passages in English. The first complete English translation, by Wang Hong and Zhao Zheng, was published in 2008 by the Sichuan People's Publishing House, and a complete German translation by Konrad Herrmann appeared in 1997. A Japanese translation of the 1166 edition was printed in three volumes by Umehara Kaoru (1978–1981).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dream Pool Essays” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1040 the artisan Bi Sheng invented printing with movable clay type, as the scholar Shen Kuo recorded in his <b>Dream Pool Essays</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
